:11.25pt;
 color:#728188'><span lang=3Den-IN>Amazon SQS FIFO queues follow exactly-on=
ce
 processing. It introduces a parameter called&nbsp;</span><span
 style=3D'font-weight:bold' lang=3Den-US>Message Deduplication ID</span><sp=
an
 lang=3Den-IN>, which is the token used for deduplication of sent messages.
 Suppose a message with a particular message deduplication ID is sent
 successfully. In that case, any messages sent with the same message
 deduplication ID are accepted successfully but aren’t delivered during t=
he
 5-minute deduplication interval.</span></p>
 <p style=3D'margin:0in'><img src=3D"dojo1_files/image014.png" width=3D606
 height=3D129></p>
 <p style=3D'margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:7pt;font-size:11.25pt'><span
 style=3D'font-family:Calibri;color:#728188' lang=3Den-IN>SQS remembers the
 MessageDeduplicationId values it’s seen for at least five minutes, which=
 means
 deduplication Ids can only reduce, not completely eliminate, the chances of
 duplication occurring. For example, if a producer was unable to receive an
 acknowledgment after sending a message due to a network issue and then reg=
ains
 connection after 10 minutes and attempts to resend the message, there is a
 risk of duplication occurring.<br>
  In this scenario, you can lessen the chances of the Lambda function proce=
ssing
 duplicate messages by storing data in an SQS FIFO queue. You may provide
 a&nbsp;MessageDeduplicationId value so SQS can distinguish one message from
 another. Optionally, you may enable&nbsp;ContentBasedDeduplication to let =
SQS
 create an&nbsp;SHA-256 hash based on the message body and use it as the va=
lue
 for&nbsp;MessageDeduplicationId.<br>
  Hence, in this scenario, the correct answer is to:&nbsp;</span><span
 style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-family:Calibri;color:green' lang=3Den-US>Add
 a&nbsp;</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-family:"Courier New";
 color:green' lang=3Den-US>MessageDeduplicationId</span><span style=3D'font=
-weight:
 bold;font-family:Calibri;color:green' lang=3Den-US>&nbsp;parameter to the&=
nbsp;</span><span
 style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-family:"Courier New";color:green' lang=3Den=
-US>SendMessage</span><span
 style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-family:Calibri;color:green' lang=3Den-US>&n=
bsp;API
 request.<br>
  </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-family:Calibri;color:#FF6600'
 lang=3Den-IN>Refactoring the Lambda function to store the message’s cont=
ent and
 dropping the incoming messages with similar content within a 5-minute peri=
od</span><span
 style=3D'font-style:italic;font-family:Calibri;color:#728188' lang=3Den-US=
>&nbsp;</span><span
 style=3D'font-family:Calibri;color:#728188' lang=3Den-IN>is incorrect beca=
use
 Lambda functions do not share data amongst themselves during a scale-up ev=
ent.
 Therefore, if a function is processing a message and another function hand=
les
 the succeeding message, it would not be able to compare if it is indeed a =
duplicate
 or not. You have to configure the SQS FIFO queue to use a Message
 Deduplication ID in order to avoid having duplicate messages.<br>
  </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-family:Calibri;color:#FF6600'
 lang=3Den-US>Configuring the Amazon SQS queue to automatically drop a dupl=
icate
 message whenever it arrives within the message’s&nbsp;</span><span
